[IRWB-022-I](/home/manuel/code/wesen/2026-11-24--compiler-workbench/.claude/worktrees/t2-sh-irwb-022-i/ttmp/2026/11/26/IRWB-022-I--lowering-tix-to-cuda-with-checked-execution-on-the-gpu-machine/index.md) is ready for review. The [design](/home/manuel/code/wesen/2026-11-24--compiler-workbench/.claude/worktrees/t2-sh-irwb-022-i/ttmp/2026/11/26/IRWB-022-I--lowering-tix-to-cuda-with-checked-execution-on-the-gpu-machine/design-doc/01-checked-tix-to-cuda-lowering.md) proposes emitting CUDA directly from the current Tix IR and checking one GPU batch per function. The existing async transformation API supports this, so no framework changes are proposed.

The runner changes would separate runtime inputs from the compile cache key, isolate each remote invocation, report toolchain identity and execution failures, and coordinate ordinary runs with timed runs through shared and exclusive GPU locks.

The contract uses `abs(got - ref) <= 2e-6 + 2e-5 * abs(ref)` for finite f64 references. The GB10 probe found different NaN encodings between CUDA and Node. All eight sequential and split-sum dot-product results matched Stage 1’s f32 words, and the worst result used **1.38%** of the proposed f64 error budget. The [diary](/home/manuel/code/wesen/2026-11-24--compiler-workbench/.claude/worktrees/t2-sh-irwb-022-i/ttmp/2026/11/26/IRWB-022-I--lowering-tix-to-cuda-with-checked-execution-on-the-gpu-machine/reference/01-implementation-diary.md) records the replay commands and evidence.

Committed locally as `c7fc6d4`. The worktree is clean. Implementation and corpus timings follow review.
